116 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 82 0 R
/E (The agenda, "Reframing Narcotics Policy: Distinguishing Addiction from Crime and Examining Capital Punishment," is an opportunity for delegates to confront a central tension in contemporary drug governance. Narcotics policy has long treated drugs as a threat to public order, national security and social discipline. Yet scientific and public health evidence increasingly describes many forms of harmful drug use as a health condition or a disease shaped by dependence, trauma, poverty, mental illness, stigma and limited access to care. This distinction does not excuse violence, trafficking or organized criminality. It requires a more precise policy vocabulary: addiction should not be treated as crime, while exploitation, corruption and large-scale trafficking should remain serious criminal justice concerns. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
117 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 82 0 R
/E (The significance of the agenda lies in its practical and moral stakes. UNODC estimates that drug markets and drug-related harms remain substantial, while many people with drug use disorders do not receive treatment \(UNODC, 2024\). At the same time, criminal justice systems in many countries still process large numbers of people for use or possession. The death penalty adds a sharper controversy because several States retain capital punishment for drug offences, while UN human rights bodies maintain that capital punishment, where it exists, must be limited to the most serious crimes, understood as crimes of extreme gravity involving intentional killing \(OHCHR, 2026\). The intention of addressing this agenda is to effectively attempt to argue for a two-track approach: health-led responses for addiction and proportionate, rights-compliant enforcement against serious criminal markets, that being said this intention is an opinion that if you do choose to advocate for as a delegate it essentially means your country advocates for the same. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
