136 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 84 0 R
/E (Modern narcotics policy evolved from early twentieth-century efforts to regulate opium and other substances into a treaty-based system of international control. The core framework rests on the 1961 Single Convention on Narcotic Drugs as amended by the 1972 Protocol, the 1971 Convention on Psychotropic Substances and the 1988 Convention against Illicit Traffic in Narcotic Drugs and Psychotropic Substances. UNODC identifies these conventions as the principal legal instruments of international drug control, designed to ensure the availability of controlled substances for medical and scientific purposes while preventing diversion and illicit trafficking \(UNODC, International Drug Control Conventions\). )
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
137 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 84 0 R
/E (Although the treaties contain both health and enforcement objectives, national implementation often leaned toward policing, incarceration and punitive deterrence. Countries adopted different models depending on legal tradition, colonial history, religion, public health capacity, crime patterns and political culture. Some imposed severe penalties for possession and trafficking; others created administrative responses for personal use while preserving criminal penalties for supply. Recent cannabis regulation and decriminalization reforms show that the international debate is no longer a simple contest between prohibition and permissiveness. It is now a debate over evidence, proportionality, treaty flexibility and human rights. )
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [5]
>>
