/T (Addiction as a Medical Condition: Scientific Basis, Public Health Approaches and Evidence )
/E (Addiction as a Medical Condition: Scientific Basis, Public Health Approaches and Evidence )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
145 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 85 0 R
/E (WHO and UNODC describe drug use disorders as complex health conditions with biological, psychological, social and environmental determinants rather than merely a moral failure or a lack of discipline \(WHO and UNODC, 2020\). Dependence can involve craving, tolerance, withdrawal, relapse and impaired control over use, often interacting with trauma, exclusion, unemployment, untreated pain or mental illness. This medical framing does not eliminate personal responsibility, but it explains why punishment by itself rarely addresses the root causes of harmful drug use. )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
146 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 85 0 R
/E (A public health response therefore prioritizes prevention, voluntary treatment, psychosocial support, medication-assisted treatment where appropriate, mental health care, harm reduction, aftercare and social reintegration. Harm reduction measures such as needle and syringe programmes, opioid agonist therapy, naloxone access, overdose prevention and HIV or hepatitis services aim to reduce death and disease among people who may not immediately stop using drugs. UNODCs World Drug Report 2024 frames the right to health in drug policy through availability, accessibility, acceptability, quality, non-discrimination and participation \(UNODC, 2024\). For delegates, the question is not whether health should replace all enforcement, but whether enforcement blocks treatment or drives vulnerable people further into risk.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
