/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 86 0 R
/T (Crime and Narcotics: The Criminalization Paradigm, Critiques and Alternative Models )
/E (Crime and Narcotics: The Criminalization Paradigm, Critiques and Alternative Models )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
158 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 86 0 R
/E (The criminalization paradigm treats drug possession, consumption, production and trafficking primarily as offences against the State. Its defenders argue that law enforcement deters use, disrupts supply, protects communities and expresses social condemnation of dangerous conduct. These claims are strongest when applied to violence, coercion, large-scale trafficking, corruption, child exploitation and money laundering. Organized drug markets can weaken institutions and endanger communities, so the State has a legitimate duty to respond. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
159 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 86 0 R
/E (The difficulty is that broad criminalization often collapses very different situations into one category. A person dependent on opioids, a teenager possessing a small quantity, a coerced courier and a violent trafficker may all enter the criminal justice system even though their culpability and social risk differ markedly. UNODC reported that in 2022 roughly seven million people were in formal contact with police for drug offences, and about two-thirds of those contacts concerned drug use or possession for use \(UNODC, 2024\). Alternative models include decriminalization of small-scale possession, diversion to treatment, administrative penalties, proportional sentencing, regulated cannabis markets and hybrid systems that reserve criminal justice resources for serious trafficking and related violence. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
