/P 87 0 R
/T (Capital Punishment in Narcotics Policy: Global Practices and Arguments for and Against )
/E (Capital Punishment in Narcotics Policy: Global Practices and Arguments for and Against )
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
168 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 87 0 R
/E (Capital punishment for drug offences is among the most controversial areas of narcotics policy. Harm Reduction International reported that 34 countries retained the death penalty for drug offences in law in 2024 and that at least 615 people were confirmed executed for such offences that year, excluding countries where secrecy prevents reliable documentation \(HRI, 2025\). Retentionist States usually justify this punishment through deterrence, incapacitation, retribution and sovereignty. They argue that drug trafficking destroys families, fuels dependence and threatens public security, and that severe penalties are necessary in vulnerable trafficking environments. )
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
169 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 87 0 R
/E (Opponents respond that the death penalty is irreversible, often discriminatory in practice and unsupported by conclusive evidence that it deters trafficking better than long imprisonment or targeted enforcement. They also argue that many people facing execution are low-level couriers, poor defendants, migrants or individuals with limited ability to expose organizers. Under contemporary human rights interpretation, drug offences do not normally meet the threshold of the most serious crimes because they do not involve intentional killing by the accused. The policy question is therefore not whether trafficking is serious, but whether execution is a necessary, proportionate and lawful response to it. )
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
