/T (International Law and Human Rights: Treaties, Conventions, ICCPR and UN Positions )
/E (International Law and Human Rights: Treaties, Conventions, ICCPR and UN Positions )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
177 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (The international drug control conventions require States to prevent diversion, regulate controlled substances and act against illicit trafficking, but they do not require capital punishment. Their stated concern for the health and welfare of humankind leaves room for proportionate penalties, prevention, treatment and rehabilitation. The 2016 UNGASS outcome document, prepared through the Commission on Narcotic Drugs and adopted by the General Assembly, contains operational recommendations on demand reduction, supply reduction, access to controlled medicines, human rights, emerging challenges, international cooperation and development \(UNODC, 2016\). )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
178 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (Human rights law places independent limits on punishment. Article 6 of the ICCPR protects the right to life and allows the death penalty only in countries that have not abolished it, and only under strict safeguards. UN human rights mechanisms have interpreted the most serious crimes threshold as limited to crimes of extreme gravity involving intentional killing, excluding drug offences. OHCHR also argues that abolition is necessary for human dignity and that the death penalty lacks proof as an effective crime-control tool \(OHCHR, Death Penalty\). Thus, a State may enforce drug laws, but it must do so through legality, due process, proportionality, non-discrimination and respect for the right to health. )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
