182 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 89 0 R
/E (Portugal is central to the reform debate because it decriminalized possession of all drugs for personal use in 2001 while retaining criminal penalties for trafficking. Possession became an administrative matter handled through Commissions for the Dissuasion of Drug Addiction, which combine legal, health and social perspectives. Reviews of the Portuguese model emphasize that the reform worked in combination with expanded treatment, harm reduction and social reintegration, and that the dramatic rise in use predicted by opponents did not materialize \(Transform Drug Policy Foundation, 2021\). Portugal therefore illustrates health- led decriminalization, not simple legalization. )
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
183 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 89 0 R
/E (Singapore represents a contrasting model. It retains strict drug laws and capital punishment for selected trafficking offences above statutory thresholds, although reforms have created limited discretion in certain courier cases. Its government argues that severity deters trafficking and protects society. Critics question whether the available evidence proves a unique deterrent effect and argue that mandatory or near-mandatory death sentencing raises concerns about proportionality, fair trial guarantees and the position of low-level couriers. Singapore is useful for debate because it presents the strongest sovereignty and deterrence argument for punitive policy. )
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
184 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 89 0 R
/E (The Philippines illustrates the dangers of treating drug control as a campaign of social cleansing. A 2020 OHCHR report found serious human rights violations linked to the anti-drug campaign, including widespread and systematic killings of alleged drug suspects and persistent impunity, with official figures then indicating at least 8,663 deaths and other estimates higher \(OHCHR, 2020\). Other jurisdictions offer additional lessons: some combine strong trafficking enforcement with treatment expansion; some regulate cannabis; and some, such as Malaysia and Pakistan, have moved to restrict mandatory capital punishment. These examples show that reform is a spectrum rather than a single template. )
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
