190 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 90 0 R
/E (Ethically, narcotics policy must balance personal responsibility, public safety and social responsibility. A person who organizes trafficking, exploits minors, launders proceeds or uses violence may deserve serious punishment. A person living with dependence, homelessness or untreated trauma requires a different response. Justice is not achieved by treating unequal cases equally; it is achieved by recognizing differences in harm, culpability and capacity for rehabilitation. )
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
191 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 90 0 R
/E (Proportionality is especially important in the debate on capital punishment. Drug trafficking may be grave, but it usually does not involve intentional killing by the accused. Execution can also reproduce inequality when those punished most harshly are poor couriers while organizers remain protected. Social responsibility requires States to address the conditions that make harmful drug use and illicit markets grow: poverty, stigma, lack of treatment, weak institutions, conflict and exclusion. Humane policy does not deny crime; it insists that punishment alone cannot solve a problem rooted in health, economics and governance. )
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
