<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 91 0 R
/E (Policy alternatives should be evaluated by outcomes: fewer deaths, reduced disease transmission, weaker organized crime power, improved treatment access, fairer sentencing, less corruption and safer communities. Harm reduction is often the most practical starting point because it reduces immediate risks without necessarily changing the legal status of drugs. Decriminalization goes further by removing criminal penalties for possession for personal use, thereby reducing stigma and allowing police to focus on serious crime. Legalization and regulation, most commonly for cannabis, may reduce illicit market profits but require strict controls on advertising, youth access, potency, taxation and commercialization. )
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
196 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 91 0 R
/E (A hybrid framework is likely to be most realistic for UNCND consensus because it allows States to adapt reforms to local conditions while preserving shared principles. It can acknowledge that Member States have different legal systems and security concerns while still setting a minimum standard of humanity and effectiveness. For example, a delegation may defend strict punishment for transnational trafficking while also supporting treatment access, diversion for personal possession and a moratorium on executions. This kind of compromise is valuable in UNCND because consensus usually depends on language that is principled but implementable. Such a framework could include the following measures: )
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
197 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 91 0 R
/E (imprisonment for serious trafficking, with judicial discretion and fair trial guarantees. )
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
198 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 91 0 R
/E (voluntary treatment, counselling, housing assistance or social services where appropriate. )
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
