217 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 94 0 R
/T (diversion. Organized criminal activity requires targeted enforcement, financial investigation and international cooperation. Capital punishment for drug offences should be replaced by proportionate sentencing because it is irreversible, difficult to reconcile with the most serious crimes threshold and vulnerable to discriminatory application. For UNCND, the strongest reframing strategy is a balanced resolution on treatment, harm reduction, proportionality, abolition or moratorium of the death penalty for drug offences, and stronger action against transnational organized crime. )
/E (diversion. Organized criminal activity requires targeted enforcement, financial investigation and international cooperation. Capital punishment for drug offences should be replaced by proportionate sentencing because it is irreversible, difficult to reconcile with the most serious crimes threshold and vulnerable to discriminatory application. For UNCND, the strongest reframing strategy is a balanced resolution on treatment, harm reduction, proportionality, abolition or moratorium of the death penalty for drug offences, and stronger action against transnational organized crime. )
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
218 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 94 0 R
/T (Delegates should also remember that effective resolutions are not only declarations of principle. They must include implementation language: capacity-building for treatment systems, technical assistance for data collection, safeguards against arbitrary detention, training for police and prosecutors, and cooperation against trafficking networks. A humane drug policy does not become weaker because it is evidence-based; it becomes stronger because it directs the harshest tools of the State toward the most harmful conduct while protecting people whose primary need is care. The best reframing of narcotics policy is therefore both realistic and principled: reduce demand through health, reduce supply through focused justice, and reduce punishment that violates dignity without improving safety. )
/E (Delegates should also remember that effective resolutions are not only declarations of principle. They must include implementation language: capacity-building for treatment systems, technical assistance for data collection, safeguards against arbitrary detention, training for police and prosecutors, and cooperation against trafficking networks. A humane drug policy does not become weaker because it is evidence-based; it becomes stronger because it directs the harshest tools of the State toward the most harmful conduct while protecting people whose primary need is care. The best reframing of narcotics policy is therefore both realistic and principled: reduce demand through health, reduce supply through focused justice, and reduce punishment that violates dignity without improving safety. )
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [2]
>>
